 "context": "<figure id=\"fig-ch11-02-02\" class=\" \"><span data-type=\"media\" id=\"fs-id1904516\" data-alt=\"This illustration shows the life cycle of fungi. In fungi, the diploid (2n) zygospore undergoes meiosis to form haploid (1n) spores. Mitosis of the spores occurs to form hyphae. Hyphae can undergo asexual reproduction to form more spores, or they form plus and minus mating types that undergo nuclear fusion to form a zygospore.\">\n<img src=\"https://openstax.org/apps/archive/20240910.161227/resources/21027c609398f9766b8ce44be47e420ed687bf3f\" data-media-type=\"image/jpg\" alt=\"This illustration shows the life cycle of fungi. In fungi, the diploid (2n) zygospore undergoes meiosis to form haploid (1n) spores. Mitosis of the spores occurs to form hyphae. Hyphae can undergo asexual reproduction to form more spores, or they form plus and minus mating types that undergo nuclear fusion to form a zygospore.\" width=\"450\" height=\"310\">\n</span>\n</figure>",
 "stimulus_html": "",
 "questions": [
  {
   "id": 507847,
   "is_answer_order_important": true,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "<p>Examine the processes illustrated in the diagram. If a mutation occurs so that a fungus is no longer able to produce a minus mating type, will it still be able to reproduce?</p>\n",
   "answers": [
    {
     "id": 995076,
     "content_html": "No, because the sexual mode of reproduction is the only mode of reproduction in fungi."
    },
    {
     "id": 995077,
     "content_html": "No, because the absence of minus mating types will disrupt biological functions in fungi."
    },
    {
     "id": 995078,
     "content_html": "Yes, the fungus will be able to reproduce asexually by the mitotic divisions of spores."
    },
    {
     "id": 995079,
     "content_html": "Yes, the fungus will be able to reproduce asexually by action of some enzymes."
    }
   ],
